/*
Cellular spaces: sample guide

`cells` partitions the current coordinate plane and evaluates content in the
selected owner's local frame. It supports five layouts and filters ownership
and shape occupancy together across pixel boundaries.

```fresco
in space cells(layout: voronoi, every: 0.16, jitter: 1.0,
               seed: 37, sampling: grid4x4, cell: tile) {
    circle(at: tile.center, radius: 0.047)
        |> fill(mix(#54dfc8, #f0bc69, tile.rand))
}
```

The usual engine declarations are required, including explicit rendering policy.

Ownership and coordinates
------------------------------------------------

| Layout | Owner | Site / motif placement |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `square` | Unit lattice square | Center of its square |
| `brick` | Square shifted half a column on odd rows | Center of the shifted square |
| `hex` | Nearest site on a staggered triangular lattice | Row pitch is `sqrt(3)/2`, odd rows shifted half a column |
| `jittered` | Original unit lattice square | Site displaced inside that square |
| `voronoi` | Nearest displaced site | One bounded, seeded site per generating square |

Coordinates are divided by `every` before ownership is evaluated. Scalar `every`
uses the same scale on both axes; a vec2 scales axes independently. Hexagons are
regular for equal scales, and stretched for unequal scales. Nearest-site distance
is measured in normalized lattice coordinates, before these scales are reapplied.
Negative coordinates use floor-based cells, including odd-row staggering.

The mapped sample coordinate is `(normalized_position - site + 0.5) * every`.
The bound cell exposes:

| Member | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `id: vec2` | Signed integer-valued generating column/row, stable across queries and frame time |
| `center: vec2` | Site in the mapped local frame: `every / 2`, suitable for `circle(at: tile.center, ...)` |
| `uv: vec2` | Local coordinate divided by `every`; can extend outside `[0, 1]` in irregular/hex cells |
| `rand: f32` | Stable `[0, 1)` value from integer mixing of the ID and seed |
| `local: vec2` | Normalized position relative to the site: `uv - (0.5, 0.5)` |
| `angle: angle` | Direction around the site in the normalized plane, in radians; zero points right and positive is counterclockwise |
| `edge_distance: scalar` | Inward distance to the nearest ownership edge, in normalized cell units |

Querying the boundary
------------------------------------------------

Every `cells` layout exposes the same geometry methods:

```fresco
let outer_track = tile.inset_distance(by: 0.037)
let pixel_track = tile.inset_distance(by: 2px)
let contact = tile.boundary_point(angle: 30deg)
```

`inset_distance` is zero on the requested inset contour and positive away from
it. Plain numbers use normalized lattice units; explicit `px` expressions use
screen pixels. Pixel conversion projects the continuous incoming coordinate
Jacobian onto each boundary normal, so stretching and rotation do not use an
incorrect single cell-wide pixel scale. The result remains a normalized distance
field. Pixel width is a local differential measurement under nonlinear transforms.
The query uses the cell's captured frame, like `tile.uv`, even in a nested space.

The field is the absolute minimum inward half-plane distance after offsetting
the faces. It is exact inside the inset polygon; outside it, especially beyond
corners, it is a contour field rather than an exact Euclidean SDF. Insets large
enough to collapse the polygon have no zero contour. Signed offsets are allowed;
an outset lies outside cell ownership and is clipped by the cellular block.

`boundary_point` intersects a ray from the site with the cell boundary and returns
local **drawing coordinates**, matching `tile.center` and suitable for `circle(at:
...)`. Angles are measured in the normalized cell plane; a vec2 `every` stretches
the result. This is an angular query, not equal-distance perimeter traversal.
The site is not necessarily the polygon centroid. Jittered cells retain square
boundaries despite their displaced sites. Voronoi queries use actual neighbor
bisectors, including for pixel insets, and emit a bounded 7×7 face search only
when queried. Regular layouts use direct formulas. Legacy `repeat` bindings do
not expose these geometry queries.

See [hex circuitry](<../90) gallery/hex_circuit.fr>) for contour lighting
that uses these methods while preserving the original compact scalar field math.

`center` is not the parent-space/world-space site. This follows the existing
`repeat(..., cell: ...)` local-center convention. Existing repeat cells also gain
`.rand` with seed zero; `hash(cell)` retains its existing behavior and is not an
alias for the new integer hash.

The nearest-site search examines a 5×5 candidate window. For jitter at most one,
each site remains within its generating square: the square containing the query
has a site at distance at most `sqrt(2)`, while any excluded square is at least
two units away. The window also covers the staggered hex lattice. Equal-distance
ties choose the first site in row-major candidate order. There is no arbitrary
unbounded Voronoi point cloud or per-frame rebuilding of random sites.

Clipping and filtering
------------------------------------------------

Only the selected owner contributes at each sample. A large circle, glow, or
other motif is clipped to its ownership region; it does not accumulate into
neighbors. Use scatter when independently overlapping instances are required.
Voronoi searches neighboring sites to establish ownership, not to composite all
their motifs. `jittered` deliberately keeps square ownership, so a shifted motif
may be clipped by its original square.

`sampling` explicitly selects one of four `CellSampling` variants:

| Variant | Positions per screen pixel |
| --- | --- |
| `center` | One point at the pixel center; no supersampling |
| `grid2x2` | 4 points on a regular 2×2 grid |
| `grid3x3` | 9 points on a regular 3×3 grid |
| `grid4x4` | 16 points on a regular 4×4 grid |

Grid points lie at the centers of equal subdivisions of the incoming screen-pixel
footprint. Odd grids are supported; `grid3x3` includes the pixel center. `center`
can alias or miss thin features. Use it to disable supersampling; zero samples
would not produce a color and is not an option. The former `samples: 4` and
`samples: 16` syntax is replaced by `sampling: grid2x2` and `sampling: grid4x4`.
Sampling variants resolve in this argument's context, so `center` elsewhere
continues to mean the coordinate anchor. Qualified forms such as
`sampling: CellSampling.center` are also accepted.

Each sample reevaluates the space
chain, owner, cell context, and body. Shape fills use inside/outside occupancy
inside this integration rather than applying analytical AA a second time. Color
is alpha-weighted across samples. Authored `px` geometry retains its original size.
The compiler emits bounded shader loops for the coverage grid and nearest-site
search, sharing one copy of the shading body across samples. Increasing the sampling grid
increases runtime work without multiplying the generated WGSL body. Search order
and tie-breaking remain row-major.
Nested cellular spaces share the outermost cellular block's grid; their sampling
arguments do not multiply its work or increase its quality. `--explain` records
the ownership model, requested outer-grid budget, and candidate count.

This is bounded numerical filtering, not exact area integration. Features smaller
than sample spacing can be missed; strongly nonlinear incoming footprints are
approximated by their pixel derivatives. A discontinuous parent mapping can already
have lost the original footprint: where possible, put preceding repeat/polar
mappings in the same chain as `cells`, so each sample reevaluates them from the
continuous input. Explicit texture sampling and procedural patterns retain their
own filtering semantics. There is no claim of perfect AA
for arbitrary shader math or automatic support for overlapping instances.

Checked constraints and scope
------------------------------------------------

- `layout`, `every`, `seed`, and `sampling` are required. The period must be positive,
  finite, and known at compile time. Runtime/pixel-dependent periods are rejected.
- `seed` is a compile-time integer in `0..65535`. It changes site placement and
  `.rand`, not the lattice ID numbering. Randomness is independent of invocation
  order and time.
- `jitter` is required for `jittered` and `voronoi`, with compile-time range `0..1`.
  Zero leaves sites centered; one permits displacement up to half a cell per axis.
  Other layouts reject the argument rather than ignoring it.
- `cell:` binds only the immediate `in space` body, matching repeat-cell scope.
  Named spaces can contain the mapping without introducing a cell binding.
- `cells` must be the final transform in its chain. Put later transforms in a
  nested `in space` block so their Jacobians are evaluated in the actual selected
  local frame. Transforming the plane before `cells` and nesting cells are supported.
- Coordinates use f32. Keep normalized lattice coordinates within the precision
  range needed by the application; very large IDs lose subcell precision. The
  signed-ID integer hash assumes coordinates fit i32. This is not an arbitrary-
  precision world-coordinate system.

Following an inset contour
------------------------------------------------

`tile.contour(inset: ...)` creates a closed path around the ownership polygon.
It works with square, brick, hex, jittered, and Voronoi cells. The default inset
is zero. Bare offsets use normalized cell units; `2px` offsets every supporting
edge inward by two screen pixels. Constant negative insets are rejected;
an animated inset is clamped to zero before clipping.

```fresco
let track = tile.contour(inset: 0.037)
let pulse = chase(along: track, speed: 20px/s, tail: 3px,
    direction: clockwise, phase: tile.rand)
let wire = band(track.distance, width: 0.5px, profile: soft)
let halo = band(track.distance, width: 3px, profile: soft)
```

The contour exposes:

- `distance`: unsigned Euclidean distance to the nearest contour segment, in
  normalized cell units. Unlike `inset_distance`, this is also Euclidean beyond
  polygon corners.
- `progress`: nearest position along the perimeter, from 0 to 1, measured by
  normalized arc length. It advances counterclockwise in the cell plane.
- `length`: perimeter in normalized cell units.
- `point(at: 0.25)`: point one quarter of the way around, in local drawing
  coordinates (the same space as `tile.center`). Parameters wrap every 1.

The start is the beginning of the edge whose outward normal has the smallest
nonnegative angle from +x. This is deterministic per cell. At an exact tie for
nearest edge, the first enumerated edge wins; progress is discontinuous on the
medial axis and at the wrap seam. A collapsed contour has zero length/progress,
an effectively infinite distance (so bands vanish), and `point` returns the
site. It contributes no chase light.

`chase` produces brightness, not a drawing layer. Choose exactly one motion source:

- `speed: 20px/s` follows the contour at twenty screen pixels per second.
  Fresco rate literals already incorporate `context(time)`; do not multiply
  them by time again. This argument accepts a displacement signal, so a bare
  constant fixes the head position rather than advancing it. Normalized rates
  such as `0.2/s` measure normalized cell distance per second.
- `lap: 6s` completes each contour in six seconds. Larger contours move faster.

`direction` is `clockwise` (default) or `counterclockwise` in the normalized cell
plane; reflected transforms also reflect that direction on screen. `phase` is
an initial offset in laps. `tail: 0.03` is a fraction of a lap; `tail: 3px` is a
screen distance. Brightness halves at the specified tail length and decays
exponentially behind the head. Pixel tails require pixel speed or a lap duration.
The perimeter and nearest-point calculation use the screen metric when pixel
motion/tails are requested, so anisotropic transforms do not change the speed.
This is the local screen metric from the incoming coordinate Jacobian; under
nonlinear/projective transforms it is a local approximation, not an integrated
screen-space curve length. Animated geometry can also move the path itself.

`band(distance, width: ..., profile: ...)` is a reusable scalar brightness mask:

- `solid` (default): full brightness up to 40% of the full width, smoothly falling
  to zero at 60% of the width.
- `soft`: exponential falloff, half brightness at half the full width.

Widths must be positive. A direct contour `distance` supports pixel widths using
the exact screen-space segment distance. Other scalar distances use their
existing coordinate units and Fresco's ordinary pixel conversion. The existing
four-argument `band(lo, hi, feather, x)` overload remains available.

All contour queries for the same inset/sample share geometry lowering. Regular
cells use four/six edges; Voronoi uses bounded loops over neighboring supporting
planes. This avoids shader-source growth from unrolling, but Voronoi contour
queries do more GPU work than regular cells. No extra rendering passes are added.


Contour type, motion modes, and the original circuit look
------------------------------------------------

`contour` is a registered geometry value type. `let outer = tile.contour(...)`
infers it; an explicit declaration is `contour outer = tile.contour(...)`.
It is neither a layer nor a runtime GPU resource. Its calculations are emitted
only when its members or a consuming helper are used.

`edge_distance` is the signed inward half-plane field of the inset boundary.
`band(outer.edge_distance, ...)` preserves the original circuit's straight-edge
falloff, including its corner treatment. `distance` remains the unsigned
Euclidean distance to the actual finite segments.

`chase` defaults to `motion: perimeter`. `motion: angular` with `lap:` or `head:` instead
follows the angle around the cell site, reproducing the original circuit's
motion. Angular motion uses fractional tails and does not accept pixel speed;
use perimeter motion for `speed: 20px/s`. The gallery intentionally uses angular
motion and normalized widths to match its original appearance at any resolution.

Regular square, brick, and hex contours with normalized insets now use a single
ordered edge walk without clipping arrays. Their normalized length is analytic.
Angular motion plus an edge-distance band needs no perimeter walk at all.
Pixel-offset and irregular contours retain the general bounded clipping path.

Editor suggestions offer named arguments after `(`, commas, spaces, and newlines,
then show in-scope contour values in `along:`. Accepting an argument name requests
its value suggestions immediately. Member and enum suggestions remain constrained
to the receiver or argument type.

`head:` supplies an explicit travel position in laps, as an alternative to `lap:`
or `speed:`. It lets related lights share an authored animation signal without
reconstructing that clock independently. The restored gallery uses this to retain
its original shared orbit and slower inner pulse exactly.

Language-wide rules: ../../LANGUAGE.md (Cellular ownership and filtering).
*/

// Change voronoi to jittered to keep square ownership with displaced motifs.
// square, brick, and hex use the same contract but omit the jitter argument.
canvas cellular_voronoi(ctx: CanvasContext) -> color {
    compose {
        fill(#101827)
        in space centered(aspect: preserve)
            .rotate(12deg)
            .cells(layout: voronoi, every: 0.16, jitter: 1.0,
                   seed: 37, sampling: grid4x4, cell: tile) {
            compose {
                fill(mix(#132b3c, #26535d, tile.rand))
                circle(at: tile.center, radius: 0.047)
                    |> fill(mix(#54dfc8, #f0bc69, tile.rand))
            }
        }
    }
}
